## 4. Systems, Kan Filling, and Univalence

How do you compose two paths `p : x ≡ y` and `q : y ≡ z` into a path `x ≡ z`? Geometrically, you have three sides of a square — the base `p` at the bottom, the constant wall `x` on the left (`i = 0`), and the wall `q` on the right (`i = 1`) — and you **fill the open box** to read off the top lid:

```
concat : {A : Type} {x y z : A} → x ≡ y → y ≡ z → x ≡ z
concat p q = λ i → comp (λ j → A) (λ j → [ i = 0 ↦ x , i = 1 ↦ q j ]) (p i)
```

Here `[ i = 0 ↦ x , i = 1 ↦ q j ]` is a **system** (`V_SYS`): a partial value defined only on the boundary faces where its face conditions hold. The operation `comp` (`O_COMP`) takes a line of types, a partial tube, and a base, and produces the far lid. Every type former (`Π`, `Σ`, `PathP`, `Glue`, `data`) has its own composition clause written *inside the language itself* (`lib/prelude.cub`).

**[Watch Kan box filling (`comp`) close a square live →](/nets?preset=kan)**

### Univalence Computes

A **Glue** type glues an equivalence `e : Equiv A B` onto the boundary of a line of types, turning any equivalence into a genuine path between types:

```
ua : {A B : Type} → Equiv A B → A ≡ B
ua e = λ i → Glue B [ i = 0 ↦ (A , e) , i = 1 ↦ (B , idEquiv) ]
```

Unlike homotopy type theory with univalence as an axiom, **transport along `ua` actually computes**! Watch boolean negation become a path `Bool ≡ Bool` and execute when we `transport` `true` across it:

```
tour-not : Bool → Bool
tour-not true  = false
tour-not false = true

tour-not-inv : (b : Bool) → tour-not (tour-not b) ≡ b
tour-not-inv true  = refl
tour-not-inv false = refl

notEquiv : Equiv Bool Bool
notEquiv = isoToEquiv tour-not tour-not tour-not-inv tour-not-inv

notPath : Bool ≡ Bool
notPath = ua notEquiv

-- Transporting `true` along the equivalence path computes to `false`!
transported-true : Bool
transported-true = transport notPath true

transport-computes : transported-true ≡ false
transport-computes = refl
```

That last line — `transport-computes = refl` — is not a postulate. The interaction net literally runs `comp` on the `Glue` type, hits `unglue` on the `i = 0` face, applies `tour-not` to `true`, and arrives at `false`.

